// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  PUBLIC PROFILE DOMAIN — P6.1: a farm in public
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
//  GAP-09 multi-file schema: new domain file. Generator + datasource
//  remain in base.prisma.
//
//  WHY A NEW FILE, AND WHY THESE THREE TOGETHER. The scoping here is
//  MIXED, and the header says so rather than claiming otherwise:
//  `FarmBrand` is tenant-scoped, `PersonProfile` and
//  `PublicFarmProfile` are global by design. `social.prisma` states
//  that its models are platform-scoped and that "there is no
//  `tenantId` here, and the RLS classification is deliberately not the
//  tenant-scoped one" — so `FarmBrand` cannot live there without
//  making that paragraph false, which is the reasoning
//  `farm-identity.prisma` already recorded for its own split.
//
//  They sit together because they are one path: an owner edits
//  `FarmBrand`, and a projection of the allowlisted subset appears as
//  `PublicFarmProfile`. Splitting them across files would mean reading
//  two to understand what publishing does.
//
//  THE PROJECTION HOLDS NO PII COLUMNS AT ALL, and that is structural
//  rather than a matter of discipline. P6.10's hardening test seeds
//  ЕГН, ЕИК, УРН and address and expects zero hits in the HTML,
//  JSON-LD, OG image and API. `FarmProfile` — the private model — has
//  `eik` and `urn` (encrypted at rest), `address`, `municipality` and
//  `settlement`. It had `egn` too until #2257 dropped the column, so
//  P6.10 now seeds one PII field fewer — there is no ЕГН anywhere to
//  leak, which is a stronger position than a projection that omits it. None of those columns EXIST on `PublicFarmProfile`,
//  so a projection writer cannot leak them by forgetting an allowlist
//  entry: there is nowhere for the value to land. The allowlist (P6.1
//  PR 2) is the second line, not the only one.
//
//  WHY THE HANDLE IS ON FarmBrand AND NOT ONLY ON THE PROJECTION.
//  Owner ruling 2026-10-14: a handle is a SEPARATE public identifier,
//  not the tenant slug. If it lived only on the projection then
//  unpublishing would free it, and P6.7 prints handles on A6 paper and
//  into QR codes — a freed handle makes printed material resolve to a
//  different farm later. Holding it on the owner's own row reserves it
//  for as long as the farm exists, published or not. That answers
//  "can a handle be reused?" with no, by construction.
//
//  Handle availability is observable through the unique constraint: a
//  taken handle fails with 23505 regardless of RLS. That is how every
//  username system behaves and is the intended behaviour here — it is
//  NOT an enumeration leak of publication state, because the
//  constraint answers "reserved" and says nothing about `published`.

/// The owner's editable source of truth for how their farm appears in
/// public. TENANT-SCOPED: one row per tenant, RLS-protected, and the
/// rls-coverage ratchet picks it up automatically from `tenantId`.
///
/// Nothing here is public by itself. Publishing is a separate act that
/// produces a `PublicFarmProfile` row; this model is what the owner
/// edits and what survives an unpublish.
model FarmBrand {
  id       String @id @default(cuid())
  /// One brand per farm. `@unique` rather than a plain index: two brands
  /// for one farm is the bug this prevents, and it is the same shape
  /// `FarmProfile.tenantId` already uses.
  tenantId String @unique

  /// The public identifier in `/f/[handle]`. Nullable because a farm
  /// exists before it chooses one, and `@unique` across every tenant
  /// because the namespace is global — see the header on why it is
  /// reserved here rather than on the projection.
  ///
  /// Reserved words, length and character rules are enforced in the
  /// application layer, not here: a CHECK constraint cannot be changed
  /// without a migration, and the rules are normalisation-dependent —
  /// `src/lib/public-profile/handle.ts` checks them against the
  /// TRANSLITERATED slug, which is how the Cyrillic spelling of a
  /// reserved word is caught.
  ///
  /// Note what is NOT reserved. A handle is the SECOND path segment, so
  /// it cannot collide with a top-level route: `/f/api` is not `/api`.
  /// An earlier revision of this comment listed `api`, `t`, `org`,
  /// `_next` and `.well-known`; reserving those would refuse real farms
  /// their own names for no routing reason. What CAN collide is a future
  /// static sibling of `[handle]` under `src/app/f/`, and a guard
  /// derives those from the route tree rather than trusting a list here.
  handle String? @unique

  /// Curated public name — deliberately NOT `FarmProfile.producerName`,
  /// which is the legal name and may be a person's.
  displayName String?
  tagline     String?
  about       String?

  /// Coarse location and crops, for P6.8's directory filters. Oblast
  /// only: `FarmProfile` holds the address, municipality and settlement,
  /// and those stay private.
  oblast String?
  crops  String[]

  /// The handle currently live in `PublicFarmProfile`, or null if this
  /// farm has never been published. PRIVATE and tenant-scoped, because
  /// it exists only so the projection writer knows which public row to
  /// remove when an owner changes their handle — without it the old row
  /// would be orphaned and `/f/[old-handle]` would keep answering.
  projectedHandle String?

  /// Publication state. `published` is the switch an owner flips;
  /// `publishedAt` records when it last became true, for the
  /// "unpublish → 404 within 60s" claim in P6.10 to be measurable
  /// against something.
  published   Boolean   @default(false)
  publishedAt DateTime?

  /// Whether search engines may index the public page — the SOURCE for
  /// `PublicFarmProfile.indexable`, which has had the column since P6.1 and
  /// no source for it since: every projected row is `false`, and a refresh
  /// deliberately leaves the column alone so a republish cannot change it.
  ///
  /// Separate from `published` because they are different decisions. An owner
  /// who shares a link with their buyers has published; that is not consent
  /// to appear in a search for their name, and collapsing the two would make
  /// the second happen silently as a side effect of the first.
  ///
  /// Ships one release BEFORE the editor that writes it, so a rollback in
  /// that window costs nothing — see this migration's `.down.sql`.
  indexable     Boolean @default(false)
  /// Per-field visibility — P6.6 (#1815), owner ruling 2026-10-15.
  ///
  /// One flag per OPTIONAL published field. The eye toggle in the editor sets
  /// these; `projectFarmBrand` will omit a hidden field from
  /// `PublicFarmProfile` while the text stays here.
  ///
  /// Hiding is NOT deleting, and that is the whole ruling. All four fields are
  /// nullable, so before this the only way to withhold «За стопанството» was
  /// to delete the paragraph and retype it to show it again — a destructive
  /// action behind an icon that means "shown/hidden".
  ///
  /// Four explicit columns rather than a `String[]` of hidden names, because
  /// #1815 asks for "a test that enumerates both and asserts they agree": a
  /// column per field makes the set of toggles STRUCTURALLY comparable to the
  /// set of published allowlist fields, so a new public field shipped without
  /// a toggle reddens. An array could be checked for membership, never for
  /// completeness.
  ///
  /// `handle` and `displayName` have no flag: both are publication
  /// PRECONDITIONS, so a published farm cannot withhold them — there would be
  /// no page to serve and no name on it. `indexable` has none either: it is a
  /// decision about crawlers, not a field a visitor reads. All three are
  /// recorded as reasoned exemptions in `NOT_HIDEABLE`, which the agreement
  /// test requires to be exhaustive.
  ///
  /// NOTHING READS THESE YET — the columns ship one release ahead of their
  /// writer, as `FarmBrand.indexable` (#1850 -> #1873) and
  /// `ExchangeListing.publishAsFarm` (#1914 -> #1922) did.
  /// P6.3 (#1812) — the farm's logo, as the CONTENT HASH of a processed image.
  ///
  /// A hash rather than a `FileRecord` FK for the same reason `crops` is a
  /// `String[]` here: `PublicFarmProfile` is tenantless and cannot hold a
  /// foreign key into tenant-scoped `FileRecord`, so the projection must carry
  /// something self-contained. A sha256 is, and it makes
  /// `/api/public/media/[hash]` immutable by construction — one hash names
  /// exactly one byte sequence, forever.
  ///
  /// Null means no logo. The bytes live in `FileRecord`, keyed by
  /// `contentSha256`.
  logoMediaHash String?

  /// Gallery photographs, ordered, as content hashes.
  ///
  /// An array rather than a join table: the cap is small and enforced in the
  /// usecase, order is the array order, and a join table would add a
  /// tenant-scoped model to classify for RLS and erasure in exchange for
  /// nothing the array does not already give.
  photoMediaHashes String[]

  hideTagline Boolean @default(false)
  hideAbout   Boolean @default(false)
  hideOblast  Boolean @default(false)
  hideCrops   Boolean @default(false)

  /// P6.6 ships a consent sheet on publish, and consent to publish
  /// identifiable information about a farm is a legal artefact rather
  /// than UI state — so it is recorded here from the start, with the
  /// version of the text that was shown.
  consentedAt        DateTime?
  consentTextVersion String?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  tenant Tenant @relation(fields: [tenantId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@index([oblast])
  @@index([published])
}

/// A person's own public identity. GLOBAL BY DESIGN, following `User`:
/// a person exists once across every farm they belong to — that is what
/// `TenantMembership` is for — so this is keyed by `userId` and carries
/// no `tenantId`.
///
/// Classified in `GLOBAL_BY_DESIGN_MODELS` alongside `User` rather than
/// left to the tenant-scoped ratchet, which would have no `tenantId` to
/// enforce on.
model PersonProfile {
  id     String @id @default(cuid())
  userId String @unique

  displayName String?
  about       String?

  /// Default OFF. A person who has never opened the editor is not
  /// published, and the absence of a row means the same thing.
  published Boolean @default(false)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  user User @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
}

/// The read model every public route uses. GLOBAL BY DESIGN —
/// cross-tenant readability IS the point, the same argument
/// `ExchangeListing` already carries.
///
/// Written ONLY through the exhaustive field allowlist (P6.1 PR 2) and
/// read ONLY via `runWithoutRls({ reason: 'public-projection-read' })`.
/// Public routes never touch tenant tables.
///
/// Every column here is already decided to be public. There are no
/// visibility flags: a field an owner has hidden is ABSENT from this
/// row, not present-and-suppressed, so a rendering bug cannot reveal
/// it and a `select *` cannot either.
model PublicFarmProfile {
  /// The handle is the identity of the read model, because `/f/[handle]`
  /// is the only way it is ever looked up. A surrogate id would add a
  /// second key nothing queries by.
  handle String @id

  /// NO `tenantId`, and that is a correction worth recording.
  ///
  /// I first carried one here so the projection writer could find its
  /// row. The rls-coverage ratchet refused it, and was right twice
  /// over: `TENANT_SCOPED_MODELS` is DMMF-derived from the PRESENCE of
  /// a `tenantId`, so the column put this model in the tenant-scoped
  /// set AND in `GLOBAL_BY_DESIGN_MODELS` — classified twice, with no
  /// policies, which is exactly the state the ratchet exists to catch.
  ///
  /// It is also wrong on product grounds. A public read model carrying
  /// the internal tenant id publishes that identifier to anyone who
  /// reads the row, which is the coupling this phase exists to avoid —
  /// the same reasoning as the owner's ruling that a handle is not the
  /// tenant slug. The writer finds its row by `handle` instead, and
  /// `FarmBrand.projectedHandle` tells it which row to remove when a
  /// handle changes.

  displayName String
  tagline     String?
  about       String?
  oblast      String?
  crops       String[]

  /// Whether search engines may index this profile. P6.5 ships robots
  /// and a sitemap covering indexable profiles only, so the flag has to
  /// be readable from the projection without consulting the owner's row.
  indexable Boolean @default(false)

  /// The Latin form of `displayName`, for P6.8's directory search.
  ///
  /// STORED rather than computed, and that is the decision this column
  /// exists to record. `transliterate()` implements the Закон за
  /// транслитерацията (2009) Streamlined System — the spelling on
  /// Bulgarian passports and road signs, so «Победа» yields `Pobeda`,
  /// which is what a farmer expects to type.
  ///
  /// The mapping is NOT injective: `a` comes from both `а` and `ъ`, and
  /// `y` from both `й` and `ь` (measured 2026-10-14). So reversing a
  /// Latin query to Cyrillic has more than one answer and a search built
  /// that way would silently miss farms. Transliterating the STORED name
  /// forward has exactly one answer per input, so the Latin form lives
  /// here and the query is never reversed.
  ///
  /// A column and not a functional index, because a trigram index over an
  /// application function would make the index depend on code the
  /// database cannot see — and the function's output would then have to
  /// stay byte-stable across deploys for the index to remain valid.
  ///
  /// Nullable: the projection writer populates it in the PR after this
  /// one, per the phase rule that tables ship a release ahead of the code
  /// that writes them. A null means a row projected before that shipped,
  /// and the only consequence is that its Latin form does not match —
  /// its Cyrillic name still does.
  displayNameLatin String?

  /// P6.3 (#1812) — the projected image hashes.
  ///
  /// These are the AUTHORITY for public media, not merely a copy. The media
  /// route serves a hash only if some projection references it, and that is
  /// what makes unpublishing revoke access: a hash is unguessable but
  /// permanent, so "a FileRecord with this hash exists" would keep serving an
  /// image after the farm withdrew it. Checking the projection ties the bytes'
  /// reachability to the same row that publishes everything else.
  logoMediaHash    String?
  photoMediaHashes String[]

  /// When the projection was last rewritten. The freshness handle for
  /// P6.10's "unpublish → 404 within 60s".
  projectedAt DateTime @default(now())

  @@index([oblast])
  @@index([indexable])
  /// P6.8 trigram search. `gin_trgm_ops` is the operator class pg_trgm
  /// installs; without it a GIN index on text is useless for similarity.
  @@index([displayName(ops: raw("gin_trgm_ops"))], type: Gin)
  @@index([displayNameLatin(ops: raw("gin_trgm_ops"))], type: Gin)
  /// Public thumbnail authorisation — #2160.
  ///
  /// `resolvePublicMedia` answers "is this hash published by somebody?" with
  /// `WHERE logoMediaHash = $1 OR photoMediaHashes has $1`, once per public
  /// image request. Neither column was indexed, so every thumbnail on
  /// `/f/<handle>` sequentially scanned this table — cheap while the table is
  /// small, and growing with the number of published farms, which is backwards
  /// for a public endpoint served `Cache-Control: immutable`.
  ///
  /// Two indexes rather than one because the columns are different shapes: a
  /// nullable scalar takes a btree, and a `String[]` needs GIN for the
  /// containment arm. A single composite would serve neither.
  @@index([logoMediaHash])
  @@index([photoMediaHashes], type: Gin)
}
